#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060534} :activity/id #uuid "10bdaba2-b2d6-5479-a66a-9f5c1d933300" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050902 :topic/id #uuid "6fa843c3-0803-5725-a7d6-d567e4f1b216" :topic/math-academy-id 281} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060534 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060897 :tutorial/content "The **second derivative**  is the derivative of a function's first derivative. It is written as $f''(x).$\n\nTo calculate the second derivative of a function $f(x)$, we first need to calculate the first derivative $f'(x).$ After that, we calculate the derivative of $f'(x).$ So using prime $\\left('\\right)$ notation, we can define the second derivative of a function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf''(x) = (f'(x))'.\n$$\n\nLet's try it out with the function $f(x) = 4x^2 + 3x.$ The first derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =(4{x}^{2}+3x)^{'} \\\\  & =4(2)x+3 \\\\  & =8x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now calculate the derivative of $f'(x)$ to give us $f''(x).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{''}(x) & =({f}^{'}(x))^{'} \\\\  & =(8x+3)^{'} \\\\  & =8+0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the second derivative of $f(x)$ is $8.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "947c462e-76d9-5248-93f8-a558f1ad16c7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2633 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9931d327-3dfa-546d-9bad-52bfcf096bda" :step/math-academy-id 12357 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060535}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060535 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059677 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059505 :question/id #uuid "0bc72287-b782-5add-89ef-f2a465573ab0" :question/math-academy-id "e-3653"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89f65f58-bd3d-5c3f-b9e0-462d74d75b59" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050420}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060172} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233123} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233601} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235430} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514409}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Second Order Derivatives"} :step/id #uuid "67b86b8b-be5c-54ba-b03c-e422581c1992" :step/math-academy-id 12358 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060536}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060536 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060898 :tutorial/content "We can also express the second derivative using $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d} x}$ notation, also known as Leibniz notation. If we have a function $f(x),$ then the second derivative $f''(x)$ can be written as\n\n$$\nf''(x) = \\dfrac {\\textrm d^2 f}{\\textrm d x^2} = \\dfrac {\\textrm d} {\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac {\\textrm df} {\\textrm d x}\\right).\n$$\n\nSometimes, we define a function as $y=f(x),$ in which case we could also express the second derivative as $\\dfrac {\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f21f7901-ec61-5c3d-a6f2-6bba03a55a17" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2634 :tutorial/title "Using Leibniz Notation"} :step/id #uuid "8fa38988-5cde-5291-bf68-d15bba187f2a" :step/math-academy-id 12359 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060537}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060537 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059678 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059506 :question/id #uuid "b092bc79-d369-5ff0-a6bb-5fc059699275" :question/math-academy-id "e-3654"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6afb4058-908a-5983-9c1e-78ee3fbadf44" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050690}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484417} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233603} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233938} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240821} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240822} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244175} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317334} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484415}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Second Order Derivatives Using Leibnitz Notation"} :step/id #uuid "65dcc063-d790-5cae-ba88-b2b94d516359" :step/math-academy-id 12360 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060538}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060538 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060899 :tutorial/content "To calculate the third derivative of the function $f(x)=2x^4+3x,$ we need to differentiate it three times.\n\nComputing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{4}+3x) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4{x}^{4-1}+1\\cdot 3{x}^{1-1} \\\\ \\text{ } & =8{x}^{3}+3{x}^{0} \\\\  & =8{x}^{3}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{''}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8{x}^{3}+3) \\\\  & =8\\cdot 3{x}^{3-1}+0 \\\\ \\text{ } & =24{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the third derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(24{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =24\\cdot 2{x}^{2-1} \\\\ \\text{ } & =48x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third derivative of $f(x)$ is $f'''(x)=48x.$ \n\nUsing Leibniz notation, the third derivative of a function $f(x)$ can also be written as\n\n$$\nf'''(x)=\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(f(x))\\right)\\right)=\\dfrac {\\textrm d^3}{\\textrm d x^3}f(x).\n$$\n\nIn general, if we want to calculate the $n$-th order derivative of a function, we need to differentiate it $n$ times:\n\n$$\nf^{(n)}(x)=\\dfrac {\\textrm d^n}{\\textrm d x^n}f(x).\n$$\n\n**Note**: The parenthesis in the superscript of $f^{(n)}(x)$ means that it is the the $n$-th derivative of $f(x).$ This is to distinguish it from $f^n(x)$ with no parenthesis, which indicates the $n$-th power of $f(x).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f1bfdba2-25dc-51af-97f8-00b3b216d0a4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2635 :tutorial/title "Higher Order Derivatives"} :step/id #uuid "2c85fed7-69ed-56ea-8cfa-78cf775d9d35" :step/math-academy-id 12361 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060539}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060539 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059679 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059507 :question/id #uuid "627f1ef4-64a8-5764-89c7-cb4e31009dbe" :question/math-academy-id "e-3655"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "322168bb-52e3-5352-a2e3-c5bdbf2bc13e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048749}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233740} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233772} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297321} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317354} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317358} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317359} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544842}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Higher Order Derivatives"} :step/id #uuid "75f9df48-9a05-5828-82cb-52a03412297c" :step/math-academy-id 12362}] :activity/title "Second and Higher-Order Derivatives" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059952}]}
